To address the above issue, we present a novel multimodal forecasting framework that integrates geometric priors and spectral feature fusion for short-term wind power prediction. We first employ a geometric encoder to explicitly model directional inductive biases by transforming raw wind vectors and nacelle angles into rotation-invariant scalar features,  improving its generalization across diverse wind regimes. Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) is flexible to adapt to varying resolutions.  We then employ an FNO-based Fusion to effectively combine the encoded turbine states with gridded weather forecasts by performing global convolutions in the frequency domain, thereby capturing complex, long-range spatiotemporal dependencies within and across modalities. Extensive experiments on three real-world wind farms and weather reanalysis data demonstrate that our framework consistently outperforms a comprehensive range of both spatio-temporal and wind power scenario generation models. Ablation studies further validate the contribution of each core component, confirming the necessity of both the geometric encoding and the neural operator fusion.



