\textbf{(1) Spatio-temporal forecasting (mostly SCADA-driven).}
This line of work models a wind farm as a set of interacting nodes (turbines) and learns spatio-temporal dependencies from historical turbine measurements.
For example, DCRNN introduces diffusion convolution on a directed graph and couples it with recurrent units to capture propagation-like spatial effects together with temporal dynamics \cite{Li2018DCRNN}.
STGCN improves efficiency by stacking graph convolutions with temporal convolutions, enabling fast spatio-temporal learning without heavy recurrent computation \cite{Yu2018STGCN}.
Graph WaveNet further learns an adaptive adjacency matrix jointly with dilated causal temporal convolutions to capture long-range dependencies and unknown graph structures \cite{Wu2019GraphWaveNet}.
MTGNN proposes a graph-learning mechanism with multi-scale temporal convolution blocks to jointly learn latent inter-node relations and temporal patterns, which has become a strong general-purpose baseline for multivariate spatio-temporal forecasting \cite{Wu2020MTGNN}.
AGCRN learns node-adaptive parameters and an adaptive graph for graph recurrent networks, improving flexibility in capturing heterogeneous node behaviors \cite{Bai2020AGCRN}.
While these models are effective for exploiting cross-turbine correlations from SCADA histories, they are often deployed in a SCADA-only setting and thus may miss upcoming synoptic/mesoscale changes that are better captured by NWP \cite{tuncar2024review}.

\textbf{(2) Wind power scenario generation / probabilistic forecasting (often NWP-driven).}
Instead of outputting a single point estimate, probabilistic methods aim to produce calibrated predictive distributions or multiple coherent future trajectories (scenarios), which are crucial for risk-aware decision making in markets and system operation.
A classical NWP-based approach is EMOS, which statistically calibrates ensemble forecasts by fitting a parametric predictive distribution to correct bias and dispersion \cite{Gneiting2005EMOS}.
ECC then reconstructs spatial-temporal dependence by coupling calibrated marginal distributions with the rank structure of the raw ensemble, yielding multivariate scenarios that preserve realistic correlation patterns \cite{Schefzik2013ECC}.
More recently, diffusion-based generative models have been developed for probabilistic time-series forecasting by learning a denoising process that can sample diverse yet coherent trajectories \cite{Rasul2021TimeGrad}.
In the wind power domain, conditional latent diffusion models have been proposed to generate short-term wind power scenarios with improved sharpness and calibration under complex uncertainty \cite{Dong2024CLDM}.
Although these NWP-driven probabilistic approaches can quantify uncertainty, many of them do not explicitly model turbine operational states and the non-stationary turbine--wind relationship, which may reduce accuracy when turbine-level effects (e.g., yaw behavior or curtailment regimes) dominate \cite{Foley2012}.

