\paragraph{SCADA (point) observations.}
At each history step, turbine $n$ provides a 7-D SCADA vector
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}^{\text{obs}}_{t,n} = [P_{t,n}, \Delta P_{t,n}, W_{t,n}, \sin\theta_{t,n}, \cos\theta_{t,n}, \sin\psi_{t,n}, \cos\psi_{t,n}] \in \mathbb{R}^{7},
\end{equation}
where $P$ is power, $W$ is observed wind speed, $\theta$ is wind direction, and $\psi$ is nacelle yaw. 
Stacking all turbines yields $\mathbf{X}^{\text{obs}}_{t} \in \mathbb{R}^{N\times 7}$, and the history tensor is $\mathbf{X}^{\text{obs}}_{t-L+1:t}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times 7}$.

\paragraph{CERRA (grid) meteorological inputs.}
For each turbine $n$, we sample $K$ neighboring grid points around its location. 
CERRA analysis features at history steps are
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}^{\text{an}}_{t} \in \mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times K\times 4},
\end{equation}
and CERRA forecast issued at anchor $t$ provides lead-wise inputs
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}^{\text{fc}}_{t} \in \mathbb{R}^{H\times N\times K\times 4},
\end{equation}
where the 4 channels correspond to $(\text{speed}, \text{direction}, u, v)$.
Each grid point also has a relative position 
$\mathbf{p}_{n,k}=(\Delta x_{n,k},\Delta y_{n,k}, d_{n,k})\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$, yielding $\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times K\times 3}$.

\paragraph{Prediction target.}
The target is the next $H$ hourly powers
$\mathbf{Y}_{t+1:t+H}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times N}$.
We use a supervision mask $\mathbf{M}\in\{0,1\}^{H\times N}$ to exclude periods affected by curtailment/downtime.

\subsection{Overall Framework}
\label{sec:overall}
Our model follows an encoder--decoder design.
A multi-modal fusion encoder maps heterogeneous inputs (SCADA point observations, CERRA analysis grids, and CERRA forecast grids) into:
(i) a history latent sequence $\mathbf{Z}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times D}$ and 
(ii) lead-wise exogenous features $\mathbf{E}^{\text{fc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times N\times E}$.
A graph recurrent decoder then predicts future power $\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{t+1:t+H}$ conditioned on $(\mathbf{Z},\mathbf{E}^{\text{fc}})$.

\subsection{Input Construction and Normalization}
\label{sec:input}
We align SCADA to hourly targets and 3-hour inputs. 
Wind direction $\theta$ and nacelle yaw $\psi$ are converted into $\sin/\cos$ pairs to avoid angle discontinuity.
All scalar inputs are standardized (z-score) using the training period and valid entries.
CERRA forecast features are normalized separately with per-dimension statistics.
Invalid entries are set to zero (with corresponding validity masks for grid features).

\subsection{Multi-Modal Spatio-Temporal Encoding}
\label{sec:encoding}
\paragraph{SCADA functional representation (FuncRepVFR).}
We encode SCADA inputs with a set-based spatio-temporal operator and a temporal spectral layer.
Given $\mathbf{X}^{\text{obs}} \in \mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times d}$ and spatio-temporal coordinates
$\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{(LN)\times 3}$ (each point has $(t,x,y)$),
FuncRepVFR outputs $\mathbf{Z}^{\text{obs}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times D}$.
This module is applied to both scalar SCADA channels ($d{=}3$) and vector-related SCADA channels ($d{=}4$).

\paragraph{Grid encoding (GridEncoder).}
For grid inputs $\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times N\times K\times f}$ with relative positions $\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times K\times 3}$,
GridEncoder projects each grid point, adds position embeddings, optionally performs set-convolution over the $K$ points, and aggregates them via attention with a distance penalty, producing 
$\mathbf{Z}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times N\times D}$.
We use this encoder for CERRA analysis at $T{=}L$ and for CERRA forecast at $T{=}H$.

\paragraph{Horizon pooling.}
CERRA forecast yields lead-wise embeddings $\mathbf{E}^{\text{fc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times N\times E}$.
We further pool them into a time-invariant node representation $\mathbf{Z}^{\text{fc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}$ by attention across the horizon dimension, and broadcast it to match the history length for fusion.

\subsection{Direction-Aware Geometric Modeling}
\label{sec:geometry}
\paragraph{Wind vector construction and misalignment.}
From SCADA wind speed and direction, we form the horizontal wind vector in the earth frame:
\begin{equation}
u_{t,n} = -W_{t,n}\sin\theta_{t,n},\quad v_{t,n}=-W_{t,n}\cos\theta_{t,n}.
\end{equation}
We also compute the nacelle--wind misalignment $\Delta\psi_{t,n}=\theta_{t,n}-\psi_{t,n}$ in a continuous form:
\begin{equation}
\cos\Delta\psi_{t,n}=\cos\theta_{t,n}\cos\psi_{t,n}+\sin\theta_{t,n}\sin\psi_{t,n},\quad
\sin\Delta\psi_{t,n}=\sin\theta_{t,n}\cos\psi_{t,n}-\cos\theta_{t,n}\sin\psi_{t,n}.
\end{equation}
These quantities are encoded in the SCADA vector branch and further used as physics contexts for fusion.

\paragraph{Wake-directed cross-turbine mixing.}
To model directional inter-turbine influence (e.g., advection and wakes), we compute a flow direction $\hat{\mathbf{f}}_{t}$ from the farm-mean unit wind vectors and build a soft upstream-to-downstream weight matrix based on along-flow and cross-flow distances between turbines.
This induces a directional message passing step on SCADA latents (residual + layer normalization) and yields wake-strength features used in fusion contexts.

\paragraph{Downwind grid-point penalty.}
To emphasize inflow-relevant grid points, we modify the distance term in the grid encoder by penalizing downwind points:
\begin{equation}
d' = d + \lambda \,\mathrm{ReLU}\big(s\cdot(\Delta x\,\hat{u} + \Delta y\,\hat{v})\big),
\end{equation}
where $(\hat{u},\hat{v})$ is the unit wind direction estimated from CERRA $u/v$, and $\lambda,s$ are learnable scalars.
The modified $d'$ is then used in the attention distance penalty inside GridEncoder.

\subsection{Frequency-Domain Tri-Branch Fusion with Vector Modulation}
\label{sec:fusion}
\paragraph{Tri-branch fusion.}
We maintain three information sources: SCADA observation, CERRA analysis, and CERRA forecast.
For each source, we obtain scalar latents $\mathbf{Z}^{\text{obs}}_s,\mathbf{Z}^{\text{an}}_s,\mathbf{Z}^{\text{fc}}_s\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times D}$ and vector latents $\mathbf{Z}^{\text{obs}}_v,\mathbf{Z}^{\text{an}}_v,\mathbf{Z}^{\text{fc}}_v\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times D}$.
We fuse them with a frequency-domain gating mechanism along the temporal dimension: rFFT $\rightarrow$ frequency-wise gating $\rightarrow$ iFFT $\rightarrow$ temporal smoothing.
For the scalar branch, the gating network is further conditioned by physics contexts (SCADA--analysis alignment and analysis--forecast alignment), implemented by injecting the contexts' spectral magnitudes into the gate input.

\paragraph{Vector-to-scalar modulation (VecFiLM).}
After obtaining the fused vector latent $\mathbf{Z}_v$, we generate modulation parameters $(\boldsymbol{\gamma},\boldsymbol{\beta})$ via an MLP and apply a FiLM-like transformation to the fused scalar latent $\mathbf{Z}_s$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z} = (1+\sigma(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\gamma})\odot \mathbf{Z}_s + \sigma(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\beta},
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\alpha)$ is a learnable global scale. The final encoder output is $\mathbf{Z}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N\times D}$, and the decoder exogenous input is $\mathbf{E}^{\text{fc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times N\times E}$.

\paragraph{Graph recurrent decoder.}
We adopt an encoder--decoder graph recurrent network. 
The encoder consumes $\mathbf{Z}$ for $L$ steps and outputs the final hidden state.
The decoder iteratively predicts $H$ future steps; at each step it takes the previous prediction and the corresponding exogenous feature $\mathbf{E}^{\text{fc}}$ as input, producing $\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{t+\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}$.

\paragraph{Training objective.}
We minimize the masked MAE:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \frac{\sum_{\tau=1}^{H}\sum_{n=1}^{N} M_{\tau,n}\,|\hat{Y}_{\tau,n}-Y_{\tau,n}|}{\sum_{\tau,n} M_{\tau,n} + \epsilon}.
\end{equation}
We report masked MAE/RMSE, and additionally summarize performance over short ($<3$ hours) and longer ($<6$ hours) horizons by slicing the prediction window.



